Now, to understand the whole setup you will need to understand how I am planning on using the opcodes. Since I low key don't know anything, 
I relied on AI and after some back and forth came up with this:

R-Type: opcode 0000, funct 4 bits, 16 instructions:
  0000 0000  ADD
  0000 0001  SUB
  0000 0010  ADC
  0000 0011  SBC
  0000 0100  AND
  0000 0101  OR
  0000 0110  XOR
  0000 0111  NOT
  0000 1000  SHL
  0000 1001  SHR
  0000 1010  SAR
  0000 1011  CMP
  0000 1100  MOV
  0000 1101  NEG
  0000 1110  LOAD   $Rdst, ($Rsrc)
  0000 1111  STORE  ($Rdst), $Rsrc

I-Type  opcodes 0001-0110, 6 instructions:
  0001  LDI    $Rdst, imm8
  0010  ADDI   $Rdst, imm8
  0011  SUBI   $Rdst, imm8
  0100  ANDI   $Rdst, imm8
  0101  ORI    $Rdst, imm8
  0110  XORI   $Rdst, imm8

I2-Type  opcodes 0111-1010, 4 instructions:
  0111  LDAT0  addr12    load RAM[addr12] into $T0
  1000  LDAT1  addr12    load RAM[addr12] into $T1
  1001  STAT0  addr12    store $T0 to RAM[addr12]
  1010  STAT1  addr12    store $T1 to RAM[addr12]

J-Type  opcodes 1011-1111, 5 slots:
  1011  J      addr12
  1100  CALL   addr12
  1101  JEQ    offset12
  1110  JNE    offset12
  1111  JLT    

So, most of this is pretty self explanatory. A lot of the common assembly instructions that are missing are pseudo-instructions. 
